uares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 & 60 \\\\ 30 & 20 & 40\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 & 60 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{3})}{1}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-137920/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-137920"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9831"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/bdc283cb936da0169970929ff4f078cc4b1ad58ae124625655e7f8d3a0a15b24.png)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A \\mathbf{x} = W\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/c149ecd1fe76187859c289443581b4b2999aa2bd1e409723253a33bee56fb100.png)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b},$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/f7cc4573dd79ddd8b872f75bae85670e72dcdcba0b2a514d9e21a27c599fc711.png)\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}29 \\\\ 54\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+54 = 104.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9832"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/2429c49383a8610e76edcee474205d1f261fc81d1ec65ac4178ebc60f3c6d8d8.png)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b},$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/6607f2839cfc50abcf9a25f0cd22319042c29009fec90ffef35e43d8f24b5be7.png)\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 \\\\ -21 & 21\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-39 \\\\ 39\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ -21 & 21 & 39\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{13}{7}+x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/c7ad0d1ea0ea307fe96ce69b9e4f046d9eab66a56bbba8de7105a3ad7e150be9.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
